Mycenaean signet ring” (CMS I, 179) whose iconography and interpretation may be 
compared to that of the Nestor Ring; potentially useful for contextual Mycenaean
signet ring iconography.

**Captured content / faithful summary:**

- The article addresses a well-known Mycenaean signet ring (CMS I, 179) from the
Tiryns treasure and analyzes its iconography and religious or mythological 
significance. [5]  
- The engraved scene on CMS I, 179 is interpreted as depicting a female deity, 
possibly Demeter, accompanied by an eagle associated with Zeus, suggesting a 
mythological or cultic context. [5]  
- The author argues that the ring’s imagery symbolizes the eternal cycle of the 
seasons, linking the scene to themes of fertility, agricultural renewal, and the
alternation of life and death. [5]  
