                                Research Report                                 

“Ring of Nestor” Mycenaean gold ring archaeology iconography                    

 • The paper is explicitly titled as a study of the iconography of the “Ring of 
   Nestor,” dealing with its imagery within the broader context of Aegean Bronze
   Age signet rings and, importantly, the issue of forgeries in this corpus. [1]
 • The author reviews the ring’s iconographic program, referencing motifs such  
   as female figures, possible deities, cult scenes, and religious symbols that 
   are often associated with Minoan and Mycenaean glyptic art. [1]              
 • The paper compares the scenes on the Ring of Nestor with securely provenanced
   Minoan and Mycenaean rings, assessing whether the composition and style fit  
   within known traditions or display anachronistic mixtures suggestive of a    
   modern forgery. [1]                                                          
 • Arthur Evans presents the “Ring of Nestor” as part of a “sepulchral treasure 
   of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia,” associating the   
   ring with a burial context in Boeotia on the Greek mainland. [2]             
 • The monograph describes the ring as a massive gold signet ring with an       
   elaborately engraved bezel depicting a complex scene interpreted by Evans as 
   showing aspects of the “Minoan after-world.” [2]                             
 • Evans argues that although the ring was found (or at least said to have been 
   found) at Thisbê in Boeotia, the style and iconography of the engraved scene 
   are essentially Minoan, suggesting connections between mainland Greece and   
   Minoan Crete. [2]                                                            
 • Evans situates the ring chronologically within the Late Minoan / Mycenaean   
   period, using stylistic comparison with other Minoan and Mycenaean gold      
   signet rings and seals. [2]                                                  
 • The title describes the object as the “Pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring,” indicating
   that the author questions its genuine Minoan origin or suggests it is an     
   imitation or forgery constructed in a Minoan-like style. [3]                 
 • The article focuses on “Egyptian iconography” present in the scenes engraved 
   on the Nestor Ring, arguing that certain motifs derive from Egyptian visual  
   culture rather than from authentic Aegean Bronze Age traditions. [3]         
 • The study compares the iconographic elements on the Nestor Ring—such as      
   boats, deities, ritual scenes, offering tables or altars, sacred trees, or   
   composite beings—with visual parallels in Egyptian art, suggesting that the  
   ring’s imagery may reflect modern scholarly knowledge of Egyptian and Minoan 
   motifs. [3]                                                                  
 • The author highlights that the Ring of Nestor combines several motifs—boats, 
   female individuals, altars, sacred trees, baetyls—that are individually known
   in Aegean glyptic but rarely, if ever, combined in such a complex narrative  
   scene, raising suspicion that the ring is a modern pastiche. [4]             
 • The chapter reviews the history of the ring’s discovery and acquisition,     
   noting that the ring lacks firm archaeological documentation and that its    
   supposed provenance from Thisbê, Boeotia, has been questioned. [4]           
 • The discussion situates the Nestor Ring within a broader pattern of 19th–20th
   century forgeries produced for the antiquities market, emphasizing the need  
   for caution when interpreting unprovenanced “masterpieces” that conveniently 
   illustrate contemporary scholarly theories. [4]                              
 • The engraved scene on CMS I, 179 is interpreted as depicting a female deity, 
   possibly Demeter, accompanied by an eagle associated with Zeus, suggesting a 
   mythological or cultic context. [5]                                          
 • The author argues that the ring’s imagery symbolizes the eternal cycle of the
   seasons, linking the scene to themes of fertility, agricultural renewal, and 
   the alternation of life and death. [5]                                       
 • It notes that the only previously known comparable gold signet ring from this
   area was the “famous Ring of Nestor,” which had been purchased by Arthur     
   Evans, implying that the ring did not come from systematic excavation. [6]   
 • The text indicates that the Griffin Warrior finds, including their rings, are
   securely excavated and have clear archaeological context, in contrast to     
   older finds like the Nestor Ring whose provenance and authenticity have been 
   debated. [6]                                                                 
 • The bibliography lists I. Pini, “Minoan and Mycenaean Decorated Gold         
   Finger-Rings,” Bulletin of the Institute of Classical Studies 42 (1997–1998) 
   210–211, and I. Pini, “The ‘Ring of Nestor’,” Oxford Journal of Archaeology  
   17 (1998) 1–13, among key references. [7]                                    

catalogue "Ring of Nestor" gold ring museum database                            

 • The article describes the Ring of Nestor as part of "a sepulchral treasure of
   gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia," and presents it as a 
   gold signet ring with imagery interpreted as offering a glimpse into the     
   "Minoan after-world." [8]                                                    
 • In discussing a signet ring in the City Art Museum of St. Louis, the article 
   references "THE RING OF NESTOR. (EVANS, PM. IV, FIG. 915b)," indicating that 
   Evans's illustration of the Ring of Nestor in Palace of Minos volume IV,     
   figure 915b, is used as a comparison. [9]                                    
 • The AJA review remarks, "While some may still harbor doubts as to the        
   authenticity of the ‘Ring of Nestor’ (no. 277)…", identifying the Ring of    
   Nestor as catalogue number 277 in the CMS volume and explicitly noting       
   ongoing doubts about its authenticity. [10]                                  
 • The IGNCA PDF lists a sequence including "(1) Signet-ring from Thisbe. (2)   
   The Ring of Nestor. V. Restoration of the design on the Ring of Nestor in the
   style of the Miniature Frescoes of Knossos …", suggesting a plate or figure  
   showing the original ring and a restored drawing modeled on Knossian         
   miniature frescoes. [11]                                                     
 • The Met Museum Perspectives essay refers to "gold signet rings known as the  
   Ring of Minos and the Ring of Nestor, and the well-known disk from Phaistos  
   on Crete… Gilliéron …", linking these named rings to Emile Gilliéron’s       
   historic images and reconstructions of Greek Bronze Age artifacts. [12]      
 • Hudler's paper cites the classic JHS article "‘The Ring of Nestor’: A Glimpse
   into the Minoan After-World and A Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings   
   and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia" as a reference when discussing contested
   Aegean signet rings. [13]                                                    
 • The article "Deconstructing a Narrative of Epiphany on the Isopata Ring"     
   notes that the "ring of Nestor (Evans PM III, 145–57)" has been included     
   among examples used to support narratives of divine epiphany, citing Evans’s 
   discussion in Palace of Minos volume III, pages 145–157. [14]                
 • The Academia.edu paper is described as presenting "an analysis of the        
   iconography surrounding the ‘Ring of Nestor’ in the context of Aegean        
   forgeries and authenticity debates," emphasizing both the ring's imagery and 
   its contested status. [1]                                                    
 • The gold signet ring known as the 'Ring of Nestor' comes from a sepulchral   
   treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals reportedly found at Thisbê      
   (Thisbe) in Boeotia and has been interpreted as depicting imagery related to 
   the Minoan afterworld. [8][11][13]                                           
 • The 'Ring of Nestor' is catalogued in the Corpus der minoischen und          
   mykenischen Siegel, Volume 6 (Ashmolean Museum), as entry number 277 and is  
   commonly cited in modern scholarship via that CMS reference. [10][1]         
 • Scholars continue to debate the authenticity of the 'Ring of Nestor'; while  
   it is incorporated into catalogues such as CMS 6 (no. 277), some authors     
   explicitly note ongoing doubts and treat it as a contested or potentially    
   forged Aegean signet ring. [10][13][1]                                       
 • The 'Ring of Nestor' has been widely reproduced and reconstructed, notably   
   through Emile Gilliéron’s historic images, and has been used in modern       
   scholarship as a key comparative example in discussions of Minoan epiphany   
   narratives and Aegean signet ring iconography. [11][12][14]                  
 • Evans’s 'Palace of Minos' volumes provide key early published documentation  
   of the 'Ring of Nestor', with the ring discussed in volume III (pp. 145–157) 
   and illustrated in volume IV (fig. 915b), and these references are repeatedly
   cited in later scholarly treatments and comparative studies of Aegean gold   
   signet rings. [9][14]                                                        

